Z||000005||AutoXer||07-15-2001||08:57 PM||||Hi gino<P>I have been autocrossing V6 fieros for about 3 years.  I am convinced that 90 % of autocross times is due to only 2 things:  Driving skill and tires.<P>Most likely the driver of the 3800 fiero had neither.  <P>Tires are the easy part.  Get some extra 14" wheels then go to <A HREF="http://www.tirerack.com" TARGET=_blank>www.tirerack.com</A>  and look at their competition tires, specifically the Kuhmo Victoracers.  They are about the best pure autocross tires you will find.<P>Driving skill is a little tougher.  Go to the exents and listen to the advise of the people that wave done this a while.  If you become serious about this sport consider taking the Evolution Performance Driving School.  It is a traveling school sponsored by local SCCA Regions.<P>Well that is all the wisdom I have.  Good luck.<BR><P>------------------<BR>"He ran out of talent about half way through the corner"||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||07-15-2001||08:58 PM||||keep trying it. photopoints been all screwed up for a week. Tinas been having same trouble||||reg||1
Z||000007||ginoferrari||07-15-2001||10:17 PM||||Thanks AutoXer I've made the tire selection. This local club won't let a novice use autox tires for some reason so I went with a set of American Racing 14" wheels with BF Goodrich Comp T/A's in 215/60 for now. Do you have any aftermarket sway bars or special suspention or shocks on your cars? <P>------------------<BR>84 2M4<BR>84 SE<BR>87 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000008||AutoXer||07-16-2001||06:51 AM||||No<P>I'm still running in the stock class.  Afraid of the competition in Street Prepared.  About the only mod you can do with stock class is Koni adjustable shocks.  I'm not sure they are worth the $ over KYBs for the Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>"He ran out of talent about half way through the corner"||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||07-16-2001||09:08 AM||||Thanks, Roger, but I'm no expert - I have participated in exactly one autocross and finished dead last in my class.  But don't be discouraged...I have an 88 Coupe (all stock except WS6 rear springs) and they put me in C Stock with the fast boys (V6 Fiero, Miata, 'doorstop' MR2).<P>But I did learn this...if you want to go faster, improve the following (IN ORDER):<P>1) Your driving<BR>2) Tires/alignment<BR>3) Suspension/brakes (within the rules, of course)<P>I haven't been back for another autocross yet, but I shaved a full 1.5 seconds off my time (36.5 to 35.0 sec) by listening to what some of the fast guys had to say and applying their knowledge.<P>If your car is in good mechanical condition, I'd say get out there and give it a shot!  What do you have to lose?||||reg||10
Z||000010||TRiAD||07-16-2001||10:48 AM||||<B>HAVE HOPE</B> <BR>One of the council members of our local AutoX club has a Formula (Street Prepared class; lowered, intake, exhaust, swaybars, adjustable shocks, race tires, etc) and is usually the <B>fastest</B> stock-bodied car there. He does VERY well, and is respected by all. <BR>You can tell when new people come out, because they see this black Formula line up with "trouble" on it's license plates, and make some ignorant "Fiero" comment. ...Then he runs 2 to 5 seconds faster than the Vette and Viper they were just "ooh"ing and "awe"ing about. Pretty funny.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ginoferrari||09-16-2001||12:21 PM||||You were all right. I drove my first autocross a couple weekends ago and did just fine. I ran in the novice class and beat all kinds of cars times. I beat some Vetts, MR2's, Camaro's, BMW's even some Porsch's. It has a lot to do with the drivers I guess. I beat the times of several of the folks in C Sport too, which is what I will eventually be running in.<P>------------------<BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid26/p23177a738ceb9d88e0b51db344f1ee79/fe3c196f.jpg">                     <BR>84 2M4<BR>84 SE<BR>87 Coupe||||reg||10
Z||000012||ChadMan||09-16-2001||09:38 PM||||Nice work Gino!  I hope to be back in action next month!||||reg||1
Z||000013||Slammed Fiero||09-16-2001||11:34 PM||||Did someone say Autocross???<P>This is my 5th year running a Fiero , I have had all sorts of suspension setups. If you can drive a Fiero fast your a good driver , it takes skill and patience to learn to control some of the fiero's tendancy.<P>A lot of the comments in other messages are dead on ..an autocross is 90% driver and tire...10% car. I have seen pontiac 6000's setup and blow away a field of cars. Look at the SCCA nationals where you have Neon's and CRX's posting times faster than Z06 Vettes!!<P>The mainthing is to keep focused on the class your in and go as far as you can withthe rules. The koni's are a huge help with any fiero , you need to make the back of the car stiff as nails. If I put my body weight (10lbs or so) on the back of my car it will NOT move. Get some adjustable upper ball  joints for the front of your car , Moog makes some nice one , this way you can dial in some more camber..<P>or..<P>join the Fiero racing list , I own / moderate it , 223 members to date with all sorts of autocrossers<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Philphine||09-17-2001||08:06 AM||||so 14's are best for auto x? right now i have all the stock sized aluminum wheels wheels (13's, 14's, and a set of '88 15's) plus some larger aftermarket wheels. which would be best and still keep me in the most novice class?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Banzai||09-17-2001||08:44 AM||||Hey Autoxer,  are you running  with the Saginaw Valley Region??  I used to run my 94 Z28 with those guys. Took a second in F Stock with Street tires at Barstow Airport. Then after lowering and many suspen. upgrades and motor upgrades they stuck me in C Prepared--yikes. I never picked up the BFG gumballs( the car was begging for them) but I sold it.  I'll be back though---with my 2m4. I agree with everyone else in this post that driver skill and tires play a big role.<BR>Anyone ever see Dave Feiner(been in Motor Trend) run in person ? He used to run F-Stock with a Cobra R.  Thought the guy must have had three feet! Good Times<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Leper||09-17-2001||03:18 PM||||Banzai, were you running at Barstow Airport  June 2000? (the 18th I think) I remember seeing a Camaro (black or dark blue) going down the runway as I was driving by the place.<p>[This message has been edited by Leper (edited 09-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jncomutt||09-17-2001||06:35 PM||||I'm trying to get back to Philphines question.  What exactly is a better wheel size to run in autocross, The 14s or a larger 16 or other for instance??||||reg||1
Z||000018||Banzai||09-18-2001||12:55 AM||||I think it was June of 99'. They run just about the same schedule every year.  Barstow is one of the bigger tracks. They do run at Grattan Raceway once a season. The Porshe Club runs all their events on open road courses and speedways. $150/meet compared to $20 for an SCCA event. Be 150 well spent at least once a season.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000019||BlackDragon||09-18-2001||01:10 AM||||how would someone locate the closest auto x track to them? i would like to race, but i have no idea where. <P>------------------<BR>WTB:CHEAP black standard fiero. no engine is fine, any model/condition gray interior is a plus. must have title||||reg||1
Z||000020||montage||09-18-2001||08:27 AM||||There is an autoX in Marquette MI this Sunday (not positive)at the old county airport. I ran my first autoX there in August, a stock formula and took 7th overall and 2nd in my class. The cool thing about this track is the course last time was 1.6 mi with times over 2 minutes and only 20 cars. It was awesome what a blast.||||reg||1
Z||000021||OcalaFiero||09-18-2001||09:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackDragon:<BR><B>how would someone locate the closest auto x track to them? i would like to race, but i have no idea where. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Go to <A HREF="http://www.scca.org/getstarted/divisions.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.scca.org/getstarted/divisions.html</A> <P>Then find the region that covers your area.<P><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>Ocala, Florida<BR>'86 2M4<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||thomas_l||09-18-2001||01:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jncomutt:<BR><B>I'm trying to get back to Philphines question.  What exactly is a better wheel size to run in autocross, The 14s or a larger 16 or other for instance??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Check the rulebook - I think in SCCA stock classes you have to run factory sized wheels(diameter, width, offset) but you can mount oversize/width tires as long as they don't interfere with body work. Larger/wider wheels will put you in Street Prepared. With that class you better not stop with the wheels and tires  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||AutoXer||09-18-2001||05:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Banzai:<BR><B>Hey Autoxer,  are you running  with the Saginaw Valley Region??<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep,  I run all the Saginaw Valley events, some of the Detroit Events and a few others.  If you were at Barstow 2 years ago you probably saw me slide off the runway and mow down a marker light with my Black 85GT.<P>Hope to see you at Barstow this Sunday.<P>AutoXer<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/eeege//RedFieroRacing.jpg">  <BR>"He ran out of talent about half way through the corner"||||reg||1
Z||000024||Avery Montembeault||09-20-2001||01:37 PM||||We have an autocross here at my college nearly every month sponsored by the schools race car club. Ive seen plenty of cars who on paper shouldnt be able to get out of thier own way totally kick a$$ on the course. last race we had, trophies went to a girl in a stock ford focus, a 1960's datsun 510 pieced together from spare parts found ina  junkyard, and my friends saturn coupe (I was in the passenger seat). The nissans, miata, and cameros couldn't even stay on the course. My friends saturn which got 2nd place raced on completely bald tires too. Everything comes down to the driver. alot of times you might not even get out of 2nd gear, so a high powered car doesnt always mean something. ||||reg||1
